Hi Krzystof!

Thanks - and I can tell you, this is quite some work. By now I have formalized a few tests which makes it a bit less work. However, from my experience you have to benchmark things in order to know. Moreover, there are "experts" out there, yes; but apparently there is not a golden rule or a standard. Otherwise, we would not see so many options out there. Different ODE systems my run with Adams-Moulton faster than with RK45, I am almost sure about that...

Sebastian

On Saturday, April 16, 2016 at 3:42:29 PM UTC+2, Krzysztof Sakrejda wrote:
> On Saturday, April 16, 2016 at 9:36:54 AM UTC-4, Sebastian Weber wrote:
> > Hi!
> >
> > I have done now a bit of benchmarking and from this I can say that RK45 is 3-5x FASTER in comparison to the Adams-Moulton from CVODES! So I would definitely recommend to keep RK45.
>
> Sebastian, I'm sure this is stuff that's "known" somewhere by some experts but it's fantastic to have careful benchmarks done in-house for Stan to help us figure out how to incorporate these algorithms. Thanks! Krzysztof
>
> > In order to double check that my approaches with ode_model (used in the Adams-Moulton) etc. is not the root cause for the slow down I also went ahead an reimplemented the odeint integrator with these building blocks. The speed of this newly implemented odeint RK45 integrator is the same as the old one. The nice thing though is that I can cut down on lines of code from ~800 to ~300 and out of the ~300 about 200 come from ode_model and decouple_ode_states. Hence, I would recommend to refactor the old code with these pieces, but surely keep RK45.
> >
> > I have the code written, but as it depends on pieces in the CVODES branch, can I just branch of that one?
> >
> > Sebastian
> >
> > (I ran the new RK45 code with the existing tests for odeint and all were good)
> >
> > On Thursday, April 14, 2016 at 10:09:27 PM UTC+2, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> > > Let's discuss this at the meeting Tuesday and see if
> > > everyone else is OK getting rid of Boost's odeint and
> > > just going with CVODES.
> > >
> > > No matter what we do with function names, the details
> > > of what's going on under the hood will be available.
> > > I think it's critical to make the high-level code to
> > > lower-level code path as transparent as possible for
> > > power users --- it's the only way to write good code
> > > in a language, be it C++ or Stan or R.
> > >
> > > - Bob
> > >
> > > > On Apr 13, 2016, at 3:02 PM, Sebastian Weber
> > > >
> > > > Hi Bob!
> > > >
> > > > Hmm... your arguments make sense. Also, thinking about how much code could go away if we remove the odeint RK45 integrator...that's quite a lot of code+testing code. From that perspective going with CVODES only is a big win, true. Ok, even though this won't cover everything I will fire off tomorrow a benchmark for a non-stiff problem which takes 2-3 days. That is a good stress test for the Adams-Moulton and the RK45 integrator.
> > > >
> > > > And 4 variants of integrate_ode are easier to read than using algorithms name for the advanced functions, also agreed. I only think that this naming scheme is not really expandable (and only experts use it, so it's fine to have the names), but maybe that is just a minor point. As I have been exposed to these things so much, I like my details; but maybe they don't matter so much in the end. Bottom line: I can live with your option A.
