Woah, just fact-checked Michael's comment:

> std::endl is not a platform-independent newline, it just adds \n and then flushes the iostream buffer.

and he's right.   The upshot is that almost all of the places
we used std::endl to construct intermediate output should just be "\n".

Now I'm confused trying to recall the tests we had that failed
on Windows with \n but succeeded with std::endl.

I saw a hint on stackoverflow that (o)fstream, but not (i)ostream,
takes "\n" (whether direct or from std::endl) and converts it to
platform-specific output.  Seems like a crazy design decision to me
and I couldn't find a trusted source to test it.

- Bob

