Yup.

>  I’d rather start with safe code that’s faster
> to write (because you know it’ll do what you tell it to) and then
> clean it up later.

It always does what you tell it to.  You just need to understand
what you're saying :-)

>> The only reason to have virtual functions is if you are going
>> to have references to the base class as arguments (i.e., you're
>> using object oriented programming).  Otherwise, it's just a useless
>> performance hit.  The compiler will let you know if you're missing pieces
>> of a necessary concept.
>
> Well, until we get the final qualifier in C++11.

How does that make a difference?

> Yes, the compiler will err out, but trying to implement a concept
> via compiler error is tremendously frustrating.

But if you have a base class with a pure virtual, you also get a compiler
error if you fail to implement the concept.   It's just that the message
is clearer (which I agree is good).

> And any doc of
> the concept then has to exist outside of the code, which is asking
> for it to fall out of date, get lost, etc.

Agreed.  And it's a huge problem.  And it's endemic to all
template-based programming and only gets worse with template
metaprograms like enable_if flying around.  It's why reading the
Boost and Eigen doc is so damn hard.

- Bob

